              <entry/>
              <entry/>
              <entry/>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </tgroup>
      </table>
      <list id="element-388" list-type="enumerated" number-style="lower-alpha">
        <item>Which value(s) of <m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:math> occurred most frequently?</item>
        <item>If you tossed the coin 1,000 times, what values could <m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:math> take on? Which value(s) of <m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:math> do you think would occur most frequently?</item>
        <item>What does the relative frequency column sum to?</item>
      </list>
    </note>
  </content>
  <glossary><definition id="randvar">
      <term>Random Variable (RV)</term>
      <meaning id="id1167466326893">a characteristic of interest in a population being studied; common notation for variables are upper case Latin letters <m:math><m:mi>X</m:mi><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mi>Y</m:mi><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mi>Z</m:mi><m:mo>,</m:mo></m:math>...; common notation for a specific value from the domain (set of all possible values of a variable) are lower case Latin letters <m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>,</m:mo></m:math> and <m:math><m:mi>z</m:mi></m:math>. For example, if <m:math><m:mi>X</m:mi></m:math> is the number of children in a family, then <m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:math> represents a specific integer <m:math><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mo>…</m:mo></m:math>. Variables in statistics differ from variables in intermediate algebra in the two following ways.
        <list id="arrvee" list-type="bulleted">
          <item>The domain of the random variable (RV) is not necessarily a numerical set; the domain may be expressed in words; for example, if <m:math><m:mi>X</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo></m:math> hair color then the domain is {black, blond, gray, brown, red}.</item>
          <item>We can tell what specific value <m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:math> the random variable <m:math><m:mi>X</m:mi></m:math> takes only after performing the experiment.</item>
        </list>
      </meaning>
    </definition></glossary>
</document>
